  "description": "Faithful replication with the original scenes: participants are randomly assigned to wild or tended (park-like) nature and see two computer-generated nature scenes of that type, one dense (many view-restricting trees) and one spacious (a wide-open landscape), in random order. Each scene is shown for about one minute; participants then rate it on four scales from the paper's Table 1, all 1-5: social aspirations (5 items, e.g. 'I would like to meet here with a friend'; the last item is reverse-scored), awe (11 items of the Awe Experience Scale), fascination (5 Perceived Restorativeness Scale items) and sense of presence (Spatial Presence Experience Scale self-location items). Age, gender and nationality are asked at the end. Rebuilt from van Houwelingen-Snippe, van Rompay, de Jong & Ben Allouch (2020), Does Digital Nature Enhance Social Aspirations? An Experimental Study, International Journal of Environmental Research and Public Health 17(4): 1454, with the four scenes cropped from its Figure 3 (CC BY 4.0, made by the authors in Unity with the Gaia package). Image ids: tended_dense, tended_spacious, wild_dense, wild_spacious; nature_type and scene_order record the assignment. Analysis as in the paper: mixed ANOVA of each scale mean with spaciousness (within) and type of nature (between), with order of appearance as a factor; the paper found spacious scenes raised social aspirations (M 3.87 vs 3.67) and awe, mainly when people watched alone. Differences from the original study: (1) still images instead of animated scenes projected 4 x 2.4 m with bird sounds and a wood scent; (2) participants always watch alone online, so the paper's social (pairs) viewing condition is not included; (3) the Likert anchor labels are ours (the paper gives only '5-point Likert scales'); (4) the paper lists 8 presence items but prints 6, and only the 6 printed items are used; English items only (the paper also used a Dutch translation); (5) the paper's manipulation checks are not described in enough detail to copy and are left out; demographic wording is ours.",
